      \field{abstract}{Molecular phylogenetics is very old and it predates by the origin of several molecular biology technologies, such as sequencing of DNA and protein sequences. Initially, the objective of phylogeny was to classify organisms according to their similarities and differences. The difficulty in obtaining large mathematical datasets when morphological characters are used was one of the main driving forces behind the gradual shift toward molecular data. Nucleic acids (DNA and RNA) and proteins are information molecules that can be compared using computer programs and estimate the evolutionary relationships based on the degree of homology between the sequences. Our objective is to describe common terminology used in molecular phylogenetics and to compare character-based, distance-based, and Bayesian inference methods. We will also explain how one can assess the level of confidence of a phylogenetic tree, using bootstrapping and jackknifing. Lastly, we also analyze various tools available for inferring evolutionary relationships among organisms.}
